```java
import java.util.Stack;

public class StockSpanner {
    public int[] stockSpanners(int[] prices) {
        Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();
        int[] result = new int[prices.length];

        for (int i = 0; i < prices.length; i++) {
            while (!stack.isEmpty() && prices[i] >= prices[stack.peek()]) {
                stack.pop();
            }

            if (!stack.isEmpty()) {
                result[i] = stack.size() + 1;
            } else {
                result[i] = i + 1;
            }

            stack.push(i);
        }

        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StockSpanner stockSpanner = new StockSpanner();
        int[] prices = {100, 80, 75, 70, 60, 85};
        System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.toString(stockSpanner.stockSpanners(prices)));
    }
}
```

This code uses a stack to store the indices of the prices array. For each price in the array, it pops elements from the stack until it finds a price that is lower than the current price or the stack becomes empty. The number of times the top element was popped plus one is the span of the current price. If the stack is empty after popping all the elements, the span is set to the current index plus one.

This solution works by maintaining two data structures:

1.  `stack`: stores the indices of the prices array.
2.  `result`: stores the spans for each day in the prices array.

The time complexity of this solution is O(n), where n is the number of days in the prices array. The space complexity is also O(n), as we need to store the indices and spans in the stack and result arrays, respectively.
